  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 235 PM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly dry with seasonable temperatures and \nsignificantly lower humidity Monday-Wednesday. Temperatures warm \nback above normal late in the week an upper ridge builds back into \nthe region. Next chance for rain Fri-Sat.\n\nA cold front has moved through the area and winds have shifted to \nthe N and increased in its wake. Some land-based obs are measuring \nwind gusts of around 20 mph. Temperatures are currently in the lower \nto mid 80s and dew points have started dropping as drier air filters \nin and are sitting between 60-70F, with a few lower 70s still \nhanging on across NE NC. As high pressure starts to build in at the \nsurface, aloft an upper level trough is moving across the NE and \nnorthern Mid-Atlantic. Simultaneously, an upper ridge across \nthe south is slow expanding northeastward as the aforementioned \ntrough lifts offshore. A few showers have developed just south \nof our NE NC counties in the Albemarle Sound right along the \nfront, but otherwise, there is minimal rainfall expected today \ndue to the incoming drier air. Satellite imagery still shows \nsome cloud cover across the area as the upper trough moves \nthrough, but most areas are still seeing partly cloudy to mostly\nclear skies behind the front. \n\nAs high pressure continues builds in behind the front tonight, winds \nwill start to diminish on land and skies will becoming mostly clear. \nWith light winds and mostly clear skies, this will be a favorable \nenvironment for radiational cooling. Dew points will drop into the \n50s for most of the forecast area, so lows will be able to drop in \nthe upper 50s to lower 60s for most locations, though low-mid 50s \nare likely in many locations to the NW of Richmond. Dew points are \nforecast to remain very comfortable through at least Thursday as \nstrong sfc high pressure (nearly 1030 mb), sets up from off the New \nEngland coast SW into the Carolina coastal plain through midweek. \nHighs will stay mostly in the low-mid 80s through Wed with lows in \nthe 50s to lower 60s (locally warmer at the immediate coast). The \nupper level ridge is forecast to expand E-NE to the TN Valley and \ncentral Appalachians Thu-Fri. Temperatures climb back to above \nnormal for mid- September, with highs generally in the mid-upper \n80s. Low end diurnal rain chances return Friday into Saturday as \nanother front approaches the region.",
